From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Subject: [PATCH] b43: drop received frames with the A-MSDU bit

The RX status has an A-MSDU bit (B43_RX_MAC_AMSDU, RXS_AMSDU_MASK in
brcmsmac) with a two-bit aggregation type next to it. b43 does not look
at it and passes such a frame to mac80211 as a complete MPDU.

On an EA6900 (BCM4360) with an ath11k client, one frame out of 6 million
in a 5 GHz upload had the A-MSDU bit, with aggregation type 1 where
every other frame had 3. Its 802.11 header was intact, but from the QoS
control field on it held unrelated data, so with hardware decryption
mac80211 read a random CCMP PN from it. That PN was stored for whichever
TID the data named, and every later frame of that TID was dropped as a
replay until the station rekeyed: the upload stopped. The client only
sends A-MSDUs at high rates, so this showed up once the upload got
faster.

Drop these frames, as brcmsmac does (brcms_c_recv(), "not supporting
A-MSDU"). With this, 30 rounds of the same upload and a 10-hour soak
with two clients had no replay.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/xmit.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/xmit.c
@@ -1349,6 +1349,14 @@
 	if ((macstat & B43_RX_MAC_DECERR) &&
 	    (dev->phy.type != B43_PHYTYPE_AC || (macstat & B43_RX_MAC_DEC)))
 		goto drop;
+	/*
+	 * A frame with the A-MSDU bit is not a complete MPDU: the one seen had
+	 * an intact 802.11 header and unrelated data from the QoS control on,
+	 * so mac80211 would read a random CCMP PN from it and drop every later
+	 * frame of that TID as a replay. Drop it, as brcmsmac does.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(macstat & B43_RX_MAC_AMSDU))
+		goto drop;
 
 	/* Skip PLCP and padding */
 	padding = (macstat & B43_RX_MAC_PADDING) ? 2 : 0;
